CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=00f1fcdb-0f068baf-010f-068baf83-00000004:None
CREATED:20261026T181552Z
DESCRIPTION:Duke English invites you to join us for the first installment 
 of our new Conversation Series. Professor Allison Neal and Duke English P
 hD candidate Tess-Brown Lavoie will discuss Professor Neal's teaching and
  her book\, American Poetic Voice in the Era of Global Broadcast: Mass Ve
 rnacular (2026). Hear her thoughts on how 20th-century sound technologies
  and mass media shaped American poetic voice.\n\nBook Description:\n\nAme
 rican Poetic Voice in the Era of Global Broadcast situates American poetr
 y within global media to reveal the broad institutional\, technological\,
  and cultural resonances of poetic voice. Reading works by Marianne Moore
 \, W.H. Auden\, Frank O'Hara\, Adrienne Rich\, and Audre Lorde\, it trace
 s how poetry intersects with specific sound technologies like the phonogr
 aph\, telephone\, radio\, and tape recorder\, and various vocal practices
  and institutions\, including elocution instruction\, poetry reading circ
 uits\, and radio-based cultural diplomacy.\n\nForging a representative Am
 erican poetic voice long posed a conceptual obstacle in a country of comp
 eting accents and languages. New twentieth-century sound technologies and
  vocal institutions produced an American voice through mass circulation. 
 American English shifted from the heterogeneous\, spoken counterpart of s
 tandardized\, literary British English to a globally heard network standa
 rd. This synthetic American voice sparked a parallel American poetic voic
 e\, also mediated through technology and global networks. Routing their p
 oetry through modern communication circuits\, American poets created a pa
 radoxically intimate\, global "mass vernacular\," envisioning lyric voice
 s circulating like other official and mass voices. In this context\, the 
 formal poetic elements we associate with the lyric-not just voice but als
 o tone\, apostrophe\, address\, and prosopopoeia-reveal a complex history
  of American cultural consolidation and global distribution. By bridging 
 the figurative and material dimensions of voice\, this book highlights th
 e competing cultural currents underlying lyric address in an age of globa
 l English.
DURATION:PT1H30M
DTSTAMP:20261104T163540Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261116T113000
LAST-MODIFIED:20261104T163540Z
