CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=8a0183a7-83184018-0184-87170e31-00006277:Hinz\, Nic
 olle
CREATED:20261023T192413Z
DESCRIPTION:Bacteria often grow in biofilms\, cell clusters held together 
 by a self-produced matrix. This lifestyle promotes physiological differen
 tiation\, making it difficult to control bacterial growth. My group studi
 es the multicellular physiology of Pseudomonas aeruginosa\, a metabolical
 ly versatile bacterium that produces diverse virulence factors and is a m
 ajor cause of biofilm-based infections. To visualize and investigate mech
 anisms underpinning differentiation in biofilms\, we use fluorescent repo
 rters and deuterium labeling paired with microscopy of thin sections and 
 live samples.\nWe have shown that biofilm subpopulations employ distinct 
 strategies\, such as using oxygen-scavenging complexes and endogenous ele
 ctron-shuttling compounds called phenazines\, to sustain metabolic activi
 ty. We also observed that such metabolic differentiation correlates with 
 increased drug tolerance. Moreover\, our characterization of P. aeruginos
 a biofilms revealed a virulence factor called the "refractile body" (R-bo
 dy) - a nanomachine that extends into a spear\, likely damaging host cell
 s from within. R-body production is stochastic within biofilms\, consiste
 nt with the idea that R-body producers disable phagocytes and thereby pro
 tect their kin from engulfment.\nOverall\, our work provides insights int
 o the communal lifestyle of bacteria and informs efforts to control biofi
 lm growth in contexts relevant to human health and society.
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20261023T192413Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261116T120000
